<span class="kicker">2026-08-13 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Slope a Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>“Slope” means two different things when it comes to a paver walkway, and mixing them up is where a lot of drainage problems start. There’s drainage slope, the slight, deliberate tilt built into every walkway regardless of terrain, which moves water off the surface and away from structures. And there’s hillside grade, the actual rise or fall of the ground itself when a walkway crosses sloped terrain. Every walkway needs the first kind. Only some walkways deal with the second.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, getting both types of slope right is part of every walkway project we build across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities. This guide covers how to properly slope a walkway for drainage on any site, plus how that changes when the walkway itself is also crossing hillside terrain.</p>
<h2><b>Drainage Slope Versus Hillside Grade</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Type</b></td><td><b>What It Is</b></td><td><b>Applies To</b></td></tr><tr><td>Drainage slope (pitch)</td><td>A slight tilt, typically 1 to 2 percent, moving water off the surface</td><td>Every walkway, flat or sloped terrain</td></tr><tr><td>Hillside grade</td><td>The actual rise or fall of the ground along the walkway’s length</td><td>Only walkways crossing sloped terrain</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A perfectly flat-looking walkway on level ground still needs drainage slope, without it, water sits on the surface after every rain instead of running off. A walkway climbing a hillside needs both, the overall hillside grade it’s following, plus a cross-slope for drainage layered on top of that grade.</p>
<h2><b>Why Every Walkway Needs Drainage Slope</b></h2>
<p>Water that has nowhere to go pools on the walkway surface, seeps into joints, and eventually works its way down into the base material, where repeated freeze and thaw cycles can push pavers up and out of position over time. A properly built drainage slope moves that water off the walking surface before it has a chance to sit and cause problems, whether the walkway is on flat ground or climbing a hill.</p>
<h2><b>How Much Slope a Walkway Actually Needs for Drainage</b></h2>
<h3><b>The Standard Range</b></h3>
<p>A drainage slope, sometimes called a cross-slope or pitch, of 1 to 2 percent is the standard target for a paver walkway. That translates to roughly a quarter inch to a half inch of drop for every 12 inches of walkway width. This is subtle enough that it’s rarely noticeable to someone walking across it, but it’s enough to reliably move water toward one side rather than letting it sit.</p>
<h3><b>Why Too Little Slope Causes Problems</b></h3>
<p>A slope below about 1 percent often isn’t enough to reliably move water, especially on a walkway with any surface texture or slight unevenness in the joints, which can create small pockets where water pools rather than continuing to flow.</p>
<h3><b>Why Too Much Slope Causes Problems</b></h3>
<p>A slope steeper than roughly 2 percent starts to become noticeable underfoot and can feel slightly off-balance to walk across, particularly for anyone with mobility concerns. It’s rarely necessary to exceed the standard range for drainage purposes alone, even in a climate with heavy seasonal rain or snowmelt.</p>
<h2><b>Direction of Slope: Where Should Water Go</b></h2>
<h3><b>Sloping Away From the Home</b></h3>
<p>For any walkway near a house, foundation, or other structure, the slope should direct water away from that structure, not toward it. This is one of the most important drainage principles in any hardscape project, and it takes priority over other slope considerations when the two might conflict.</p>
<h3><b>Sloping Toward a Planned Drainage Point</b></h3>
<p>Beyond just moving away from structures, ideally the slope directs water toward an intentional destination, a lawn area, a garden bed, a drain, or a lower point in the yard designed to handle runoff, rather than just wherever the walkway happens to lean.</p>
<h3><b>Cross-Slope Versus End-to-End Slope</b></h3>
<p>A cross-slope, tilting from one side of the walkway to the other, is the standard approach for most walkways, since it moves water off to the side without affecting how level the walkway feels along its length. An end-to-end slope, tilting from one end of the walkway to the other, is generally reserved for walkways that are already dealing with some natural grade change, rather than being introduced deliberately on flat ground.</p>


