<h2><b>Building Drainage Slope Into a Flat Site</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Establish Your High and Low Sides</b></h3>
<p>Before excavating, determine which side of the walkway will be the high side and which will be the low side, based on where you want water to ultimately go.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Excavate With the Slope in Mind</b></h3>
<p>Rather than excavating to a single uniform depth across the full width, excavate slightly deeper on the low side than the high side, matching your target 1 to 2 percent cross-slope.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Compact the Base Following the Same Slope</b></h3>
<p>As you add and compact your gravel base in layers, maintain that same slight cross-slope throughout, rather than compacting to a perfectly flat plane and hoping the sand layer will correct it later.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Screed the Sand Bed to the Slope</b></h3>
<p>When screeding your bedding sand, set your screed rails at heights matching the intended cross-slope, rather than level rails, so the sand bed itself carries the correct pitch before pavers go down.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Lay Pavers Following the Established Plane</b></h3>
<p>As pavers go down, check that they’re following the established sloped plane consistently, using a level held at an angle matching your target slope, rather than checking for a perfectly level result.</p>
<h2><b>Building Slope on a Walkway That Also Crosses a Hillside</b></h2>
<p>When a walkway is climbing or descending a hillside, it needs to manage the overall hillside grade along its length while still maintaining a cross-slope for drainage across its width. This means the base and sand layers are doing two jobs at once, following the general downhill direction while also tilting slightly side to side. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-paver-walkway-on-a-slope/"> how to build a paver walkway on a slope</a> covers the excavation, base depth, and drainage planning specific to hillside terrain in more depth, including when a stepped design becomes necessary rather than a continuous gradual slope.</p>
<h2><b>How to Verify Your Slope Is Correct After Installation</b></h2>
<h3><b>The Water Test</b></h3>
<p>The simplest way to confirm a walkway’s drainage slope is actually working is to run a hose across it, or wait for the next real rain, and watch where the water goes. It should move steadily off the surface toward your intended direction, without pooling anywhere along the way.</p>
<h3><b>Checking With a Level and a Shim</b></h3>
<p>For a more precise check, place a level across the walkway’s width and use a shim of known thickness, matching your target slope, under the low side. If the level reads flat with that shim in place, your slope is tracking correctly. Repeat this check at several points along the walkway’s length, since a slope that’s correct at one end can drift off target by the other end if it wasn’t consistently maintained during construction.</p>
<h3><b>Watching for Standing Water After Rain</b></h3>
<p>In the weeks following installation, keep an eye on the walkway after a few real rain events. Any spot where water noticeably lingers longer than the surrounding surface points to a low spot or inconsistency in the slope that’s worth addressing before it becomes a recurring issue.</p>
<h2><b>Common Slope Mistakes on Paver Walkways</b></h2>
<ul><li>Building a walkway perfectly flat with no drainage slope at all, assuming pavers alone will handle water</li><li>Sloping water toward the house instead of away from it</li><li>Inconsistent slope along the walkway’s length, correct in some sections and off in others</li><li>Confusing drainage slope with hillside grade and only addressing one when both apply</li><li>Exceeding the standard 1 to 2 percent range unnecessarily, making the walkway feel uneven underfoot</li></ul>
<h2><b>Why Getting Slope Right Matters More in Colorado’s Climate</b></h2>
<p>Water that isn’t properly directed off a walkway surface becomes a bigger problem specifically during freeze and thaw cycles, common along the Front Range through the winter and spring. Water sitting in low spots or pooling against joints expands as it freezes, which can push pavers out of position or contribute to base erosion over repeated cycles. A properly built drainage slope is one of the simpler, more preventable factors in keeping a walkway performing well through Colorado’s seasonal weather patterns.</p>
<h2><b>When to Bring in a Professional for Slope Planning</b></h2>
<p>Getting drainage slope right on a flat site is a manageable task for a careful DIYer with the right tools and attention to detail. It becomes considerably more demanding when a walkway is also navigating hillside grade, connecting to an existing patio or driveway at a different elevation, or dealing with a yard that has multiple drainage considerations feeding into the same area. </p>
<p>In these more complex situations, professional grading experience helps avoid a walkway that looks fine on installation day but develops drainage problems within the first year.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of planning and building properly sloped walkways across a wide range of site conditions in the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design pays close attention to getting both drainage slope and hillside grade right from the start. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see examples of properly drained, long-lasting hardscape work.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>How much slope does a paver walkway need for drainage?</b></h3>
<p>A standard drainage slope of 1 to 2 percent is recommended, roughly a quarter inch to a half inch of drop for every 12 inches of walkway width.</p>
<h3><b>Does a flat walkway on level ground still need slope?</b></h3>
<p>Yes. Even on completely flat terrain, a walkway needs a deliberate drainage slope, or water will pool on the surface rather than draining away.</p>
<h3><b>Which direction should a walkway slope?</b></h3>
<p>Generally away from any nearby structure, like a house or foundation, and ideally toward an intentional drainage point rather than wherever the terrain happens to lean.</p>
<h3><b>How is drainage slope different from a hillside walkway’s grade?</b></h3>
<p>Drainage slope is a slight cross-slope for moving water off the surface, needed on every walkway. Hillside grade is the actual rise or fall of the terrain the walkway is built on, which only applies to sloped sites.</p>
<h3><b>How can I check if my walkway’s slope is working correctly?</b></h3>
<p>Run a hose across the surface or observe it during real rain, watching for water that moves steadily off the surface without pooling anywhere along the way.</p>
<h3><b>What happens if a walkway doesn’t have enough slope?</b></h3>
<p>Water pools on the surface and works into the joints and base material, which can lead to freeze-thaw damage, shifting pavers, and base erosion over time.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Properly Sloped Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Getting drainage slope right is a subtle but important detail that affects how well a walkway performs for years after installation, on flat ground or sloped terrain alike.</p>
<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has been planning and building properly sloped, well-drained walkways across Denver, Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities for over 15 years. If you’d like your site assessed for proper drainage planning,<a href="/contact/"> reach out for a free quote and consultation</a>.</p>

